  "summary": "Handheld cameras, natural light, no props: cinema stripped by contract.",
  "description": "Dogme 95 was a manifesto with teeth: von Trier and Vinterberg's 1995 \"Vow of Chastity\" banned artificial light, props, sets, genre music, optical filters and credit-list vanity — shooting only on location with handheld cameras and ambient sound. The Celebration (1998) and The Idiots (1998) proved the aesthetic: grainy, immediate, actor-forward films whose constraints manufactured emotional rawness. Thirty-plus certified Dogme films (Julien Donkey-Boy, Italian for Beginners) followed before the movement wound down in 2005. Its legacy is the normalized digital-naturalism of festival drama — handheld, available-light, imperfect — and a demonstration that constraint breeds style.",
  "culturalContext": "Dogme 95 proved constraint breeds intimacy: the Vow's bans manufactured rawness in Festen and The Idiots, normalized handheld available-light realism across festival cinema, and the manifesto-with-certificates remains the reference point for every \"rules\" filmmaking movement since.",
